\section{Time and the lattice}{

\code{M} is the number of lattice steps per unit of the \code{time} column -- not
per survey interval, and not per year unless \code{time} is in years. A survey
at \code{time} sits at step \code{M * (time - t0)}, which must be a positive whole
number, so \code{M} has to be a multiple of whatever divides the reporting
interval: quarterly surveys in year units need \code{M} divisible by four.
Nothing requires the surveys to be equally spaced, or the demes to share
a calendar.
\code{t0} must be strictly earlier than the first survey, because the
introduction time is supported on steps \verb{1..T} and step 0 is not reachable.
The default \code{t0 = min(time) - 1} gives the introduction a year of support
before anything is observed.
}

\section{What is rejected, and why}{

\describe{
\item{\code{n == 0}}{Dropped with a message rather than an error: survey
extracts routinely carry rows for a district-year that was not
sampled, and they carry no information.}
\item{non-integral steps}{An error. Annual data at \code{M = 16} is fine;
sub-annual data at small \code{M} is not, and silently rounding it would
move observations in time.}
\item{two surveys on one step}{Reachable only with \code{snap = TRUE}, and
then an error by default. Pooling them is often right, but it is a
decision, so it needs \code{on_collision = "sum"}.}
\item{duplicate \verb{(deme, time)}}{Always an error. This is a defect in the
input, not a resolution question.}
\item{a deme with no usable surveys}{An error, naming it.}
}
A deme whose surveys are all zero is kept: it is the informative case for
the question this package exists to answer.
